            <ArticleTitle>Existing and emerging therapies for managing constipation and diarrhea.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>158-166</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1471-4892(17)30104-2</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.coph.2017.10.015</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Functional bowel disorders (i.e., constipation and diarrhea) are characterized by abdominal pain, bloating, distention, and/or bowel habit abnormalities in the absence of obvious anatomic or physiologic abnormalities on routine diagnostic tests. These symptoms are attributable to gastrointestinal sensorimotor dysfunctions resulting from peripheral and/or central mechanisms. Available drugs target the underlying bowel disturbance (i.e., constipation, diarrhea, or both), supplemented when necessary by management of pain. Osmotic and stimulant laxatives, secretagogues, and serotonin 5-HT4 receptor agonists are approved for treating constipation. Loperamide, anticholinergic agents, rifaximin, bile-acid binding agents, eluxadoline, and clonidine are used to treat diarrhea. Several exciting new compounds, some of which have been evaluated in humans, are currently under development.</AbstractText>
